            "theoryHtml": "\n<div class='module-content'>\n    <h4>1. South African Milieu and Evolution of Gandhian Satyagraha</h4>\n    <p>Gandhi arrived in South Africa in 1893. He encountered institutionalized apartheid-style discrimination targeting three categories of Indians: indentured labourers (predominantly Tamil and Telugu), ex-indentured labourers living as small traders or artisans, and affluent Meman Muslim merchants.</p>\n    \n    <h4>2. Two Phases of Gandhian Struggle in South Africa</h4>\n    <ul>\n        <li><strong>Moderate Phase (1894–1906):</strong> Relied on constitutional petitions, memorials to the Natal Legislature and British Colonial Office, and public opinion building. Founded <em>Natal Indian Congress</em> (1894) and <em>Indian Opinion</em> (1903).</li>\n        <li><strong>Satyagraha Phase (1906–1914):</strong> Shifted to active mass non-violent resistance. Key triggers:\n            <ul>\n                <li><em>Asiatic Registration Act (1906):</em> Required Indians to carry registration certificates with fingerprints at all times ('Black Act'). Gandhi formed the Passive Resistance Association.</li>\n                <li><em>Immigration Restriction Act:</em> Prohibited Indian inter-provincial migration. Satyagrahis crossed Transvaal-Natal borders defying permits.</li>\n                <li><em>Invalidation of Marriages (1913):</em> South African Supreme Court invalidated all marriages not performed according to Christian rites or registered with the Registrar of Marriages, insulting Indian women who joined the struggle in large numbers.</li>\n                <li><em>£3 Poll Tax:</em> Imposed on all ex-indentured labourers. Miners in Newcastle struck work and marched en masse into Transvaal ('Great March' led by Gandhi).</li>\n            </ul>\n        </li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h4>3. Settlements & Communes</h4>\n    <ul>\n        <li><strong>Phoenix Settlement (1904):</strong> Located near Durban, established on 100 acres. Practical experiment in community living, manual labour, and publishing <em>Indian Opinion</em>.</li>\n        <li><strong>Tolstoy Farm (1910):</strong> Set up near Johannesburg with German architect Hermann Kallenbach. Provided sustenance to families of imprisoned satyagrahis and imparted vocational training.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h4>4. Return to India (January 9, 1915)</h4>\n    <p>Gandhi returned permanently to India at the age of 45 on January 9, 1915. He was awarded the <em>Kaisar-i-Hind</em> gold medal by the British government for his ambulance work in the Boer War. He established the <strong>Satyagraha Ashram</strong> at Kochrab near Ahmedabad in May 1915, later shifted to the banks of the Sabarmati River in 1917.</p>\n</div>\n",
