            "theoryHtml": "\n<div class='module-content'>\n    <h4>1. Champaran Satyagraha (1917) — First Civil Disobedience</h4>\n    <p>European planters in Champaran (Bihar) bound ryots by contract under the <em>Tinkathia system</em> to cultivate indigo on 3/20th of their total landholding. When German synthetic indigo rendered natural dyes unprofitable, planters sought to release tenants from contracts by imposing exorbitant rent enhancements (<em>Sharahbeshi</em>) and illegal cesses (<em>Abwabs</em>).</p>\n    <ul>\n        <li><strong>Arrival:</strong> Rajkumar Shukla persuaded Gandhi to visit Champaran in April 1917. When the District Magistrate ordered him to leave under Section 144, Gandhi defied the order, choosing to undergo imprisonment—the first instance of Civil Disobedience in India.</li>\n        <li><strong>Inquiry & Outcome:</strong> Lieutenant Governor Sir Edward Gait appointed the <em>Champaran Agrarian Enquiry Committee</em> with Gandhi as a member. The Tinkathia system was abolished by the Champaran Agrarian Act 1918, and planters refunded 25% of the extracted monies.</li>\n        <li>Rabindranath Tagore conferred the title <strong>'Mahatma'</strong> on Gandhi following this success.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h4>2. Ahmedabad Mill Strike (1918) — First Hunger Strike</h4>\n    <p>In early 1918, Ahmedabad mill owners sought to withdraw the plague bonus (up to 75% of wages) introduced during the 1917 epidemic, while workers demanded a 50% dearness allowance due to wartime inflation. Owners offered only 20%.</p>\n    <ul>\n        <li>Gandhi intervened at the request of Anasuya Sarabhai. He advised workers to demand a 35% wage increase and adhere strictly to non-violence.</li>\n        <li>To strengthen workers' morale, Gandhi undertook his <em>first hunger strike</em>. On the fourth day, the mill owners agreed to submit the dispute to an arbitration tribunal, which awarded the full 35% wage increase.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h4>3. Kheda Satyagraha (1918) — First Non-Cooperation</h4>\n    <p>Due to widespread drought and crop failure in Kheda (Gujarat) in 1918, agricultural output fell below 25% of normal. According to the Land Revenue Code, farmers were entitled to complete revenue suspension. The colonial administration rejected appeals and seized livestock and lands.</p>\n    <ul>\n        <li>Gandhi assumed the spiritual leadership, while <strong>Vallabhbhai Patel</strong> and Indulal Yagnik organized village-level non-cooperation, touring villages and urging farmers to pledge non-payment of taxes.</li>\n        <li>The government conceded by directing officials to recover land revenue only from well-to-do landowners while exempting poor peasants.</li>\n    </ul>\n</div>\n",
