            "title": "Rowlatt Act (1919), Satyagraha Sabha & The Jallianwala Bagh Massacre (April 13, 1919)",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n<div class='module-content'>\n    <h4>1. The Rowlatt Act (March 1919)</h4>\n    <p>Officially designated the <em>Anarchical and Revolutionary Crimes Act of 1919</em>, it was drafted on the recommendations of the Sedition Committee headed by British judge Sir Sidney Rowlatt. All non-official Indian members of the Imperial Legislative Council (including Mohammad Ali Jinnah, Madan Mohan Malaviya, and Mazhar-ul-Haq) voted against it and resigned in protest when it was passed.</p>\n    <p>The Act suspended Habeas Corpus, allowed trials in camera without jury, and permitted preventive detention up to two years without legal representation or appeal ('No Dalil, No Vakil, No Appeal').</p>\n\n    <h4>2. Satyagraha Sabha & Nationwide Hartal</h4>\n    <p>Gandhi organized the <strong>Satyagraha Sabha</strong> in Bombay, enlisting youth and Home Rule League members. He proclaimed an all-India general strike (hartal), fasting, and prayer on April 6, 1919. It witnessed unprecedented Hindu-Muslim solidarity, as Swami Shraddhanand addressed Muslims from the pulpit of Jama Masjid in Delhi.</p>\n\n    <h4>3. The Jallianwala Bagh Massacre (April 13, 1919)</h4>\n    <p>On April 10, Amritsar's beloved leaders <strong>Dr. Saifuddin Kitchlew</strong> and <strong>Dr. Satyapal</strong> were arrested and secretly deported. Protesting crowds were fired upon, causing several deaths and retaliatory arson.</p>\n    <ul>\n        <li>On April 13 (Baisakhi Day), thousands of men, women, and children gathered at Jallianwala Bagh to protest the arrests and celebrate the harvest festival, unaware of a martial law gathering ban.</li>\n        <li>Brigadier-General Reginald Dyer sealed the narrow entrance with troops and armoured cars and ordered 1,650 rounds of rifle fire into the trapped crowd until ammunition was exhausted.</li>\n        <li>Official Hunter Committee figures claimed 379 dead; Indian national estimates placed fatalities over 1,000.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h4>4. Repercussions and Inquiries</h4>\n    <ul>\n        <li><strong>Renunciation of Honours:</strong> Rabindranath Tagore renounced his Knighthood declaring that <em>'the time has come when badges of honour make our shame glaring in their incongruous context of humiliation'</em>. Gandhi returned his Kaisar-i-Hind medal.</li>\n        <li><strong>Hunter Committee:</strong> Appointed in October 1919. Included three Indian members (Sir Chimanlal Harilal Setalvad, Pandit Jagat Narayan, and Sardar Sultan Ahmed Khan). The majority report merely characterized Dyer's actions as an 'error of judgment'.</li>\n        <li><strong>Udham Singh:</strong> In 1940, Udham Singh shot dead Sir Michael O'Dwyer (then Lt. Governor of Punjab) at Caxton Hall, London.</li>\n    </ul>\n</div>\n",
