            "theoryHtml": "\n<div class='module-content'>\n    <h4>1. The Khilafat Issue and Hindu-Muslim Unity</h4>\n    <p>Following World War I, the British dismembered the Ottoman Empire under the harsh <em>Treaty of Sèvres</em> (1920) and stripped the Turkish Sultan of his status as the Caliph (Khalifa). Indian Muslims launched the Khilafat agitation led by the Ali Brothers (Mohammad Ali and Shaukat Ali), Maulana Abul Kalam Azad, and Hakim Ajmal Khan.</p>\n    <p>Gandhi persuaded the Congress that supporting the Khilafat cause was a moral imperative and an opportunity to cement Hindu-Muslim fraternity <em>'which would not arise in a hundred years'</em>.</p>\n\n    <h4>2. Approval at Calcutta & Ratification at Nagpur (1920)</h4>\n    <ul>\n        <li><strong>Calcutta Special Session (September 1920):</strong> Chaired by Lala Lajpat Rai. Gandhi secured approval for the Non-Cooperation program until the Punjab wrongs and Khilafat injustice were remedied and Swaraj was attained.</li>\n        <li><strong>Nagpur Annual Session (December 1920):</strong> Chaired by C. Vijayaraghavachariar. C.R. Das moved the resolution. The session transformed the INC into a mass political movement:\n            <ul>\n                <li>Goal redefined as attainment of <em>Swaraj</em> by peaceful and legitimate means.</li>\n                <li>Formation of <strong>Congress Working Committee (CWC)</strong> of 15 members to function as a round-the-year executive.</li>\n                <li>Reorganization of Provincial Congress Committees on a linguistic basis.</li>\n                <li>Reduction of annual membership fee to 4 annas (25 paise) to enable peasant entry.</li>\n            </ul>\n        </li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h4>3. Program and Spread of Non-Cooperation</h4>\n    <ul>\n        <li><strong>Boycott:</strong> Foreign cloth was boycotted and publicly burnt in huge bonfires; imports of foreign cloth dropped by more than half between 1920 and 1922. Liquor and toddy shops were aggressively picketed.</li>\n        <li><strong>National Institutions:</strong> Students boycotted government schools, giving rise to national colleges: <em>Kashi Vidyapeeth</em>, <em>Gujarat Vidyapith</em>, <em>Jamia Millia Islamia</em> (initially at Aligarh), and <em>Bihar Vidyapeeth</em> (inaugurated by Gandhi at Patna in 1921).</li>\n        <li><strong>Tilak Swaraj Fund:</strong> Launched to finance charkhas and workers; oversubscribed to Rs 1.05 crore within six months.</li>\n        <li><strong>Regional Dimensions:</strong> Peasant agitations in Awadh (Baba Ramchandra) and Eka movement (Madari Pasi); Akali movement in Punjab for clearing corrupt Mahants from Gurdwaras; forest satyagrahas in Andhra Pradesh (Alluri Sitarama Raju); and Moplah Rebellion in Malabar (August 1921).</li>\n    </ul>\n</div>\n",
