            "theoryHtml": "\n<div class='module-content'>\n    <h4>1. Ideological Schism: Swarajists vs No-Changers</h4>\n    <p>Following the withdrawal of Non-Cooperation and Gandhi's imprisonment in 1922, a fierce debate erupted within the Indian National Congress regarding future political strategy:</p>\n    <ul>\n        <li><strong>Pro-Changers (Swarajists):</strong> C.R. Das, Motilal Nehru, Hakim Ajmal Khan, and Vitthalbhai Patel argued that political dormancy demoralized the masses. They advocated contesting the upcoming elections under the GoI Act 1919 to enter legislative councils, 'end or mend' them, and use obstructionist politics to expose the sham of dyarchy.</li>\n        <li><strong>No-Changers:</strong> C. Rajagopalachari (the leading theoretician), Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel, Dr. Rajendra Prasad, and Dr. M.A. Ansari insisted on boycotting councils, arguing that legislative participation diluted the revolutionary spirit and diverted energy from constructive village work (charkha, national schools, anti-untouchability).</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h4>2. Foundation of the Swaraj Party (1923)</h4>\n    <p>At the <strong>Gaya Session (December 1922)</strong>, Congress rejected the council entry resolution. Consequently, C.R. Das resigned from the Congress presidency and, along with Motilal Nehru, announced the formation of the <strong>Congress-Khilafat Swarajya Party</strong> on January 1, 1923, with Das as President and Nehru as General Secretary.</p>\n    <p>To avert a disastrous 1907-style split, a Special Session was convened at Delhi in September 1923 under Maulana Abul Kalam Azad, which permitted Congressmen to contest elections as Swarajists.</p>\n\n    <h4>3. Achievements in the Legislature</h4>\n    <ul>\n        <li>In the November 1923 elections, the Swarajists captured 42 of the 101 elected seats in the Central Legislative Assembly, achieving absolute majorities in the Central Provinces and becoming the largest party in Bengal.</li>\n        <li>In coalition with the Independent group (led by Jinnah) and Liberals, they repeatedly outvoted the Treasury benches, rejected the Finance Bill, and moved adjournment motions.</li>\n        <li>In August 1925, <strong>Vitthalbhai Patel</strong> was elected the first Indian President (Speaker) of the Central Legislative Assembly.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h4>4. Belgaum Session (1924) & Decline of Swarajism</h4>\n    <p>Following his release in 1924, Gandhi sought to reunite the party. At the <strong>Belgaum Congress Session (1924)</strong>—the only session presided over by Mahatma Gandhi—the Gandhi-Das Pact was ratified, endorsing Swarajists to operate as the legislative organ of the Congress.</p>\n    <p>However, after C.R. Das's sudden death in June 1925, the party fragmented. A faction known as the <em>Responsivists</em> (M.M. Malaviya, Lala Lajpat Rai, N.C. Kelkar) advocated accepting office and cooperating with the government to protect communal Hindu interests.</p>\n</div>\n",
