            "title": "HSRA (1928), Bhagat Singh, Saunders Assassination, Central Assembly Bombing & Lahore Conspiracy",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n<div class='module-content'>\n    <h4>1. Reorganization: From HRA to HSRA (1928)</h4>\n    <p>On September 8–9, 1928, young revolutionaries from across North India convened at the historic ruins of <strong>Feroz Shah Kotla, Delhi</strong>. Under the ideological leadership of <strong>Bhagat Singh</strong> and the military leadership of <strong>Chandrashekhar Azad</strong>, the party was rechristened the <strong>Hindustan Socialist Republican Association (HSRA)</strong>. This signaled a definitive shift from individual assassination toward mass socialist revolution.</p>\n\n    <h4>2. The Saunders Assassination (December 17, 1928)</h4>\n    <p>On October 30, 1928, during peaceful protests against the Simon Commission in Lahore, British Police Superintendent James A. Scott ordered a brutal lathi-charge, personally assaulting veteran leader <strong>Lala Lajpat Rai</strong> on the chest. Rai declared: <em>'The blows struck at me today will be the last nails in the coffin of the British Empire.'</em> He succumbed to injuries on November 17, 1928.</p>\n    <p>To avenge this national insult, Bhagat Singh, Shivaram Rajguru, Sukhdev, and Azad ambushed Assistant Superintendent of Police <strong>John P. Saunders</strong> (mistaking him for Scott) outside the police headquarters in Lahore on December 17, 1928, shooting him dead.</p>\n\n    <h4>3. Central Legislative Assembly Bombing (April 8, 1929)</h4>\n    <p>In early 1929, the colonial government sought to enact two draconian legislations: the <em>Public Safety Bill</em> (to detain communist organizers without trial) and the <em>Trade Disputes Bill</em> (to ban strikes). The HSRA resolved to register a dramatic protest.</p>\n    <ul>\n        <li>On April 8, 1929, <strong>Bhagat Singh</strong> and <strong>Batukeshwar Dutt</strong> entered the visitors' gallery of the Central Legislative Assembly in New Delhi and hurled two low-intensity bombs into an empty space behind the treasury benches, followed by red leaflets proclaiming: <em>'It takes a loud voice to make the deaf hear!'</em></li>\n        <li>They deliberately made no attempt to escape, offering themselves for arrest while chanting <strong>'Inquilab Zindabad'</strong> to utilize the court as an ideological rostrum.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h4>4. The Epic Lahore Hunger Strike & Martyrdom</h4>\n    <p>In prison, Bhagat Singh and comrades initiated an indefinite hunger strike demanding that they be treated as political prisoners with access to literature, newspapers, and hygienic food. <strong>Jatindra Nath Das (Jatin Das)</strong> attained martyrdom on September 13, 1929, after fasting for 63 heroic days, creating an outpouring of national grief.</p>\n    <p>On March 23, 1931, Bhagat Singh, Sukhdev Thapar, and Shivaram Rajguru were secretly hanged in Lahore Central Jail a day ahead of schedule, immortalizing their names in the pantheon of Indian freedom fighters.</p>\n</div>\n",
            "pointsToRemember": [
                "Transformation to HSRA (September 1928): At Feroz Shah Kotla, Delhi, Chandrashekhar Azad, Bhagat Singh, Sukhdev, and Bejoy Kumar Sinha reorganized HRA into the 'Hindustan Socialist Republican Association' (HSRA), explicitly incorporating socialism into their manifesto.",
                "Bhagat Singh's intellectual foundation: Founded the 'Naujawan Bharat Sabha' in Lahore (1926) and co-edited 'Kirti' with Sohan Singh Josh. Advocated scientific socialism and secular anti-imperialism ('Inquilab Zindabad').",
                "Assassination of J.P. Saunders (December 17, 1928): To avenge the fatal police lathi-charge on Lala Lajpat Rai by Superintendent J.A. Scott during the anti-Simon Commission protest, Bhagat Singh, Rajguru, and Azad mistakenly shot Assistant Superintendent J.P. Saunders outside Lahore police headquarters.",
                "Central Legislative Assembly Bombing (April 8, 1929): Bhagat Singh and Batukeshwar Dutt threw two non-lethal smoke bombs and leaflets into the Central Assembly hall in Delhi from the visitors' gallery.",
                "Purpose: Not to kill, but 'To make the deaf hear' (quoting French anarchist Auguste Vaillant) and protest against the reactionary Public Safety Bill and Trade Disputes Bill.",
                "Court Statement: Bhagat Singh defined revolution not as the cult of the bomb and pistol, but as the restructuring of society to end 'exploitation of man by man and nation by nation'.",
                "Historic 63-day Hunger Strike: Political prisoners in Lahore Central Jail struck work demanding humane treatment as political prisoners (access to books, proper food, sanitation). Jatin Das attained martyrdom on the 63rd day of his hunger strike (September 13, 1929).",
                "Lahore Conspiracy Case Verdict: Special Tribunal sentenced Bhagat Singh, Sukhdev, and Rajguru to death. They were hanged on March 23, 1931 (Shaheed Diwas) at Lahore Central Jail.",
                "Azad's Martyrdom: Chandrashekhar Azad was surrounded by police at Alfred Park, Allahabad, on February 27, 1931; he fought heroically to the last bullet and shot himself to keep his vow of dying 'Azad' (free)."
            ],
