            "theoryHtml": "\n<div class='module-content'>\n    <h4>1. Master Da and the Indian Republican Army</h4>\n    <p>In Bengal, revolutionary activity reached its zenith under <strong>Surya Sen ('Master Da')</strong>, a dedicated schoolteacher at Chittagong. Inspired by the 1916 Irish Easter Rising, he formed the <strong>Indian Republican Army (Chittagong Branch)</strong> alongside trusted lieutenants Ananta Singh, Ganesh Ghosh, Lokenath Bal, and Ambika Chakraborty.</p>\n\n    <h4>2. The Raid of April 18, 1930</h4>\n    <p>On Good Friday, April 18, 1930, Surya Sen executed a coordinated offensive:</p>\n    <ul>\n        <li>Revolutionaries dressed in military khaki stormed the Police Armory at Dampara and the Auxiliary Forces Armory at Pahartali.</li>\n        <li>They dismantled the telephone and telegraph exchanges and tore up railway tracks, cutting off Chittagong completely from the British administration.</li>\n        <li>Surya Sen hoisted the National Tricolor and declared a <strong>Provisional Revolutionary Government</strong>.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h4>3. Battle of Jalalabad Hill (April 22, 1930)</h4>\n    <p>Heavily armed British troops and the Eastern Frontier Rifles surrounded the revolutionaries entrenched on <strong>Jalalabad Hill</strong> on April 22. In the ensuing pitched battle, twelve revolutionaries were martyred (including Harigopal Bal / Tegra), while inflicting severe casualties on colonial forces before dispersing into the countryside to wage protracted guerrilla warfare.</p>\n\n    <h4>4. Valour of Bengal's Women Revolutionaries</h4>\n    <ul>\n        <li><strong>Pritilata Waddedar:</strong> On September 24, 1932, she commanded a 15-member team that raided the racist European Club at Pahartali. After being wounded, she swallowed potassium cyanide, becoming the first woman martyr of Bengal's revolutionary movement.</li>\n        <li><strong>Kalpana Datta:</strong> Prepared gun cotton, transported underground explosives, and operated as Surya Sen's key aide.</li>\n        <li><strong>Santi Ghosh & Suniti Choudhury:</strong> Two adolescent schoolgirls who entered the office of Comilla District Magistrate C.G.B. Stevens on December 14, 1931, presenting an administrative petition, and shot him dead.</li>\n        <li><strong>Bina Das:</strong> On February 6, 1932, during the convocation ceremony of the University of Calcutta, she stepped forward to receive her Bachelor's degree and fired five revolver shots at Governor Sir Stanley Jackson.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h4>5. Execution of Surya Sen (January 1934)</h4>\n    <p>After evading capture for nearly three years, Surya Sen was arrested at Gairala village in February 1933 following a betrayal. In jail, he was subjected to unspeakable torture (his teeth were smashed, nails extracted, and bones broken) before being hanged on January 12, 1934. His body was put in a metal cage and dumped into the Bay of Bengal.</p>\n</div>\n",
