            "theoryHtml": "\n<div class='module-content'>\n    <h4>1. The Second Round Table Conference (1931)</h4>\n    <p>Gandhi sailed to London aboard the <em>SS Rajputana</em> in August 1931 as the sole official representative of the Indian National Congress. In London, the conservative imperialists led by Sir Samuel Hoare and Winston Churchill were determined to stonewall Indian constitutional demands.</p>\n    <p>The conference bogged down in communal acrimony. Minorities (Muslims, Sikhs, Christians, Europeans, and Depressed Classes led by <strong>Dr. B.R. Ambedkar</strong>) drafted a joint Minorities Pact demanding separate communal electorates. Gandhi contended that the Depressed Classes were an indivisible limb of Hindu society and warned that separate electorates would perpetuate untouchability forever. The conference ended in complete deadlock.</p>\n\n    <h4>2. Repression & MacDonald's Communal Award (1932)</h4>\n    <p>Returning to Bombay in December 1931, Gandhi found Viceroy <strong>Lord Willingdon</strong> unwilling to negotiate. When the Congress resumed Civil Disobedience in January 1932, Willingdon enacted sweeping emergency ordinances, arrested Gandhi, outlawed Congress, and jailed over 100,000 freedom fighters.</p>\n    <p>On August 16, 1932, British Prime Minister <strong>Ramsay MacDonald</strong> announced the <strong>Communal Award</strong>, providing separate electorates not only for religious minorities but also for the 'Depressed Classes', allocating them 71 reserved seats where only Dalits could vote.</p>\n\n    <h4>3. Gandhi's Fast and the Historic Poona Pact (September 1932)</h4>\n    <p>Viewing the Communal Award as a British ploy to Balkanize Hindu society, Gandhi began a fast unto death in <strong>Yerwada Central Jail</strong> on September 20, 1932. Intense nationwide alarm prompted leaders like Madan Mohan Malaviya, Tej Bahadur Sapru, and C. Rajagopalachari to initiate negotiations with Dr. B.R. Ambedkar.</p>\n    <p>On <strong>September 24, 1932</strong>, the historic <strong>Poona Pact</strong> was signed between Dr. B.R. Ambedkar (on behalf of the Depressed Classes) and Madan Mohan Malaviya (on behalf of Caste Hindus):</p>\n    <ul>\n        <li><strong>Abolition of Separate Electorates:</strong> The system of separate electorates for Depressed Classes was discarded in favour of joint electorates.</li>\n        <li><strong>Substantial Increase in Reserved Seats:</strong> In provincial legislatures, seats reserved for Depressed Classes were increased from 71 under MacDonald's Award to <strong>148 seats</strong>.</li>\n        <li><strong>Central Legislature:</strong> 18% of the total general seats in the Central Legislative Assembly were reserved for the Depressed Classes.</li>\n        <li><strong>Educational & Welfare Grants:</strong> Adequate financial provisions were guaranteed for education and public recruitment of Depressed Classes.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h4>4. Third RTC & The Harijan Movement</h4>\n    <p>The Third RTC (November–December 1932) was a hollow session attended by only 46 delegates, resulting in the British White Paper of 1933. Gandhi withdrew from active politics to launch his <strong>Harijan Tour</strong> (1933–34), walking thousands of miles across India to fight the evil of untouchability and establishing the <em>Harijan Sevak Sangh</em>.</p>\n</div>\n",
