            "title": "Government of India Act 1935: All-India Federation, Provincial Autonomy & Dyarchy at Centre",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n<div class='module-content'>\n    <h4>1. Origins and Background of the 1935 Act</h4>\n    <p>Following the recommendations of the Simon Commission (1930), the deliberations of the three Round Table Conferences (1930–32), the British White Paper (1933), and the Joint Select Committee report chaired by Lord Linlithgow, the British Parliament enacted the <strong>Government of India Act 1935</strong> in August 1935. It was the most voluminous statute ever drafted by Westminster.</p>\n\n    <h4>2. Core Constitutional Architecture</h4>\n    <ul>\n        <li><strong>Proposed All-India Federation:</strong> To comprise 11 British Indian provinces, 6 Chief Commissioner's provinces, and those Princely States that acceded voluntarily. Since rulers representing at least half of the princely population refused to sign Instruments of Accession, the federal portion never took effect.</li>\n        <li><strong>Provincial Autonomy:</strong> The flawed system of dyarchy in the provinces was scrapped. Provinces were granted autonomous legal status with responsible executive ministries answerable to elected provincial legislatures.</li>\n        <li><strong>Dyarchy at the Centre:</strong> Executive authority was divided into <em>Reserved Subjects</em> (Defence, External Affairs, Ecclesiastical, Tribal) controlled exclusively by the Governor-General, and <em>Transferred Subjects</em> administered with the advice of an Indian Council of Ministers.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h4>3. Division of Powers & Key Institutions</h4>\n    <table class='table'>\n        <tr><th>List</th><th>Subject Items</th><th>Jurisdiction</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Federal List</strong></td><td>59 items (External Affairs, Armed Forces, Currency, Railways)</td><td>Central Legislature</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Provincial List</strong></td><td>54 items (Police, Public Health, Education, Agriculture)</td><td>Provincial Legislatures</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Concurrent List</strong></td><td>36 items (Civil/Criminal Law, Labour, Trade Unions)</td><td>Both (Federal overrides)</td></tr>\n    </table>\n    <p>Crucially, <strong>residuary legislative powers</strong> were assigned neither to the Centre nor the provinces, but vested in the sole discretion of the <strong>Governor-General</strong>.</p>\n    <ul>\n        <li><strong>Federal Court:</strong> Established on October 1, 1937, in Delhi to resolve inter-provincial disputes and constitutional questions. Sir Maurice Gwyer was its first Chief Justice.</li>\n        <li><strong>Reserve Bank of India:</strong> Established in April 1935 to manage monetary policy and currency issuance.</li>\n        <li><strong>New Provinces & Territorial Changes:</strong> Burma was severed from India in 1937; Sindh was carved out of Bombay; Orissa was separated from Bihar.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h4>4. Nationalist Critique</h4>\n    <p>Nationalists rejected the Act because real power remained concentrated in the hands of British Governors and the Viceroy through extraordinary discretionary powers, vetoes, and emergency rule (Section 93). Jawaharlal Nehru famously criticized it as <em>'a new charter of bondage'</em> and compared it to <em>'a machine with strong brakes, but no engine'</em>.</p>\n</div>\n",
            "pointsToRemember": [
                "Context: The longest and most intricate piece of legislation passed by the British Parliament until then, based on the Simon Commission report, Round Table Conferences, and the 1933 White Paper.",
                "Three Pillars of GoI Act 1935:",
                "1. All-India Federation: Proposed to unite British Indian Provinces and Princely States into a single federal union. (Never came into existence because princely rulers refused to surrender sovereignty).",
                "2. Provincial Autonomy: Abolished dyarchy in the provinces (established under GoI Act 1919) and introduced full provincial autonomy. Governors acted on the advice of responsible Indian ministers.",
                "3. Dyarchy at the Centre: Reserved subjects (Defence, External Affairs, Ecclesiastical Affairs, Tribal Areas) administered exclusively by Governor-General; Transferred subjects administered by Council of Ministers.",
                "Threefold Legislative Lists: Introduced Federal List (59 items), Provincial List (54 items), and Concurrent List (36 items). Residuary powers were vested in the Governor-General at his discretion.",
                "Bicameralism: Introduced bicameral legislatures in 6 out of 11 provinces (Bengal, Bombay, Madras, Bihar, Assam, and United Provinces).",
                "Federal Court: Established a Federal Court at Delhi (inaugurated in October 1937, headed by Chief Justice Sir Maurice Gwyer).",
                "Reserve Bank of India (RBI): Provisioned to regulate currency and credit of the country (established in 1935).",
                "Separation of Burma & Creation of New Provinces: Burma was formally separated from British India in 1937; two new provinces—Sindh (separated from Bombay) and Orissa (separated from Bihar and Orissa)—were created.",
                "Franchise: Extended voting rights based on property, tax, and education to about 14% of the adult population (approx. 35 million people). Separate electorates retained and extended to women, labour, and scheduled castes.",
                "Nationalist Verdict: Jawaharlal Nehru described the Act as 'a machine with strong brakes, but no engine' and a 'charter of bondage'. Jinnah condemned it as 'thoroughly rotten, fundamentally bad, and totally unacceptable'."
            ],
